it's great to welcome bob kuttner to the  program today who is co-editor of the  american prospect magazine  also chair at the heller school for  social policy and management  at brandeis university i really  appreciate your time today thanks for  being on  pleasure to be with you so i've been  interested in in the idea of economic  nationalism for some time  and one of the i guess where we can  maybe start is whether there is some  inconsistency among some on the left on  this issue because  i'm still developing sort of my my sense  of this but  there is a sort of uh global  egalitarianism  that i see from many who like me  describe themselves as being on the left  and progressives  that seems to stop short when it comes  to well we should have an economy that  prioritizes  the american worker we should have trade  deals that are best for  the united states is there a conflict  or an inconsistency there not  necessarily  and i think it's it's really important  to start out by defining what we mean  and i think economic nationalism means  two different things that sometimes get  uh conflated rather sloppily so good  let's start with that  please so if you are a roosevelt  progressive  and you're not a neo-liberal and you  think that  capitalism is predatory and in order to  have a system that is efficient  and socially just the government has to  be  involved big time a democratic  government has to be involved big time  in regulating capitalism  that means that you have to put limits  on globalism  because if you have what we've had for  the past 30 or 40 years with wto  and the use of trade deals to undercut  the regulation of capitalism  you can't regulate capitalism i mean  banks can go offshore  and uh we don't care whether  products that we import are made with  slave labor you you know the whole  litany  sure so economic nationalism in one  sense of the word  simply means that we have to regulate  capitalism  and we for better or worse is the nation  state there's no global economy you  can't  regulate uh capitalism with the republic  of nafta  you have to regulate it with the united  states of america where where i'm a  citizen and you're a citizen  and to some degree is that also just the  practicality of  uh legal framework and and just sort of  the the limits of what  any one group can do i mean i know we  have we have nato we have w  you know we have these organizations but  it does get sloppy  well it's about sovereignty yeah if you  want to try and regulate capitalism  you you need a degree of sovereignty and  the state is sovereign  um and uh there is no sovereignty  globally except to the extent that you  have treaties  right the other kind of economic  nationalism  of which trump is the extreme example or  maybe hitler's the more extreme example  is america first or germany first and  screw the rest of the world  and so where it gets tricky is on the  question of how you  make it possible for the whole world to  regulate capitalism  in the interest of ordinary people and  uh  since we don't have a global government  that means you have to have agreements  you have to have treaties this morning's  new york times reports that  the eu is going to have climate tariffs  if you if you try to export stuff uh  into the eu that uh  are made with processes and products  that pollute  you're gonna have to pay a tariff now  that's a good kind of nationalism  and um biden's talking about doing the  same thing  so the tricky part for me anyway  because i'm you know i'm a third world  guy as well as a new dealer  how do you extend this so that the whole  world is able to regulate predatory  capitalism  and interestingly um that is what was  attempted in 1944 at the bretton woods  conference  where the architect of that conference  was john maynard keynes he got a do-over  uh you know they got it wrong at the  treaty of versailles in  uh in 1919 where he was a junior  economic advisor to the british  government  he quit in disgust in protest  and then 25 years later he was the chair  of the bretton woods conference and he  said  we have to orchestrate the rules of the  global system  so that every country gets to regulate  capitalism  in the interests of workers in the  interest of citizens  now here's the tricky part you get down  to cases  so if we think that china is predatory  we we think that china violates human  rights we think that  china violates the rules of the trading  system where they subsidize and they  dump and they uh steal intellectual  property and all that stuff  and um so we're going to have some  we're going to have an industrial policy  and we're going to have a national  security policy where  certain technologies we're not going to  allow china to acquire  that seems pretty sensible to have an  industrial policy where do you draw the  line  and is that an industrial policy for the  united states or is that an industrial  policy that the eu  and all of the democracies are part of  and then if that's  the case um who gets the business so for  instance um there are three major  chip companies that are not chinese that  make state-of-the-art ships  one's intel the other two are european  ericsson and nokia  so do you have a cartel do you carve up  the market  or do you say we have one set of rules  for companies that  play by you know democratic norms and  and market norms and a different set of  rules for china which doesn't play by  market norms so  where it gets tricky is around the edges  where when you get down to cases  and here's vietnam a  third world country a dictatorship but  not as nasty a dictatorship as china  and a lot of stuff gets outsourced to  vietnam  by american companies and these are very  poor people who need the work  this is where it gets tricky and uh so  you have to have trade agreements that  are very different  from the kind of trade agreements that  clinton and obama made  that have human rights in them that have  some concern for the 