s  uh you you want third world countries to  be able to  uh get jobs and and gain market share  but you don't want them to exploit their  workers so let me give you an example of  a trade deal that i think was a pretty  good trade deal yeah  um there used to be something called the  multi-fiber agreement  it was a worldwide cartel for textiles  and apparel  and the idea was that um we're going to  give every  country that wants to export uh textile  products into the united states  a quota and the economist said this is  terrible this is a cartel this violates  the free market and the other side of  the argument was well if you  if you just eliminate it china will get  everything  because china will underprice everybody  and all these other countries will get  the short end of the stick common  argument huh  commonly heard commonly repeated yes so  what they did for a while this was in  the last two years of clinton when bob  rice was secretary of labor  and actually had some influence they  made they made  an agreement cambodia was the model  country and the agreement  was if if you follow  international labor organization norms  and you give workers the right to  organize  we will give you uh additional  quota slots under the multi-fiber  arrangement  and so thanks to that  the labor movement got a foothold in  cambodia  and workers actually started being paid  better it didn't hurt their market share  it helped their market share  and then of course the republicans come  in and they just blow away the whole  thing  so there's a middle ground i guess this  is my punchline yeah  there's a middle ground between  absolutely free trade which is a mirage  anyway  and um trumpian economic nationalism  which is a kind of negotiated trade  where you have some norms where you have  some standards  and the standards are not just a covert  way of screwing the third world  and a lot of the third world diplomats  have made the argument  that the west is hypocritical the west  